00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:36,180
of like an upper middle class-y thing?

266
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:38,740
But more importantly, people have come

267
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:42,800
up with a very creative way of testing for cultural bias.

268
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:45,980
And it's been done on a massive scale by the US military,

269
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:50,260
which actually is the main organization on earth that uses

270
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:54,100
IQ tests for making decisions about especially what kind

271
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:55,540
of job they're going to train you for.

272
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:59,960
All right, so I will tell you the theory of how you might go

273
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,500
about testing your cultural bias,

274
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:04,960
and then I will tell you what the tests wind up saying.

275
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:11,100
All right, and just to give you a hint, this is one

276
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:13,620
where people's intuition is usually exactly the opposite

277
00:11:13,620 --> 00:11:18,340
of the truth, so I will first go and tell you what you think,

278
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,440
and then unless you are really, if you've got rational

279
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:23,440
innovations, then you'll believe exactly what I'm going

280
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:25,220
to tell you, but otherwise I'll have to fix it.

281
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:28,380
Any case, so the way that people came up with

282
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:30,220
for testing cultural bias is this.

283
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:33,820
Let's go and on the x-axis get your IQ score.

284
00:11:33,820 --> 00:11:36,980
It could be scored in any paper and pencil test.

285
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:43,240
All right, but then over here we're going to have a test

286
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:46,400
of your practical performance, so like practical test performance,

287
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:50,700
which if you're in the military, could be something

288
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:52,800
like not crashing a plane.

289
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:55,160
All right, now I trust you'll agree

290
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:57,720
that there's nothing cultural about whether you crashed a

291
00:11:57,720 --> 00:11:59,200
plane or not, right?

292
00:11:59,620 --> 00:12:01,500
It is just an obvious fact

293
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:02,820
about whether a plane's been crashed or not.

294
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:05,300
It could, of course, be something less drastic

295
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:09,200
like how many cones do you knock over when you were driving your

296
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:10,360
tank through an obstacle course?

297
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:12,820
Of course, you want to knock over no cones.

298
00:12:12,820 --> 00:12:14,820
You want to drive your tank perfectly through the course.

299
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:19,020
So this could be any test of your skills.

300
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:21,640
So this could be a medical test where you are,

301
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:23,680
where you have to perform CPR correctly.

302
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:26,920
So anyway, on this y-axis you have any kind of task

303
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:29,460
where you can measure it independently

304
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:30,500
of the paper and pencil test.

305
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:33,200
Logically, someone could have a fantastic IQ

306
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:34,540
and they could crash the plane, right?

307
00:12:35,660 --> 00:12:37,680
Or you could have a really low IQ

308
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:38,860
and you could be a fantastic pilot.

309
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:40,020
These things are all possible.

310
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:42,660
But empirically, what does it look like?

311
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:47,780
Well, now empirically, as you might guess, when you go

312
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:50,520
and do this, you see a pretty clear correlation.

313
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:52,800
When people with higher IQ scores actually do better

314
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:55,220
on practical tasks, especially

315
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:56,300
after there's been some training.

316
00:12:57,960 --> 00:12:59,080
Right? And of course, the military,

317
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:01,400
U.S. military is maybe the biggest single job trainer

318
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:02,980
in the entire world economy.

319
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:07,160
So a lot of what they do is take people who are unskilled

320
00:13:07,380 --> 00:13:08,360
and they train them to do a job.

321
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:10,900
Right? Okay.

322
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:11,700
All right.

323
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:14,840
So and of course, that means that they need to decide how

324
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:17,140
to allocate the training to people because training someone

325
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:18,820
to be a pilot is really expensive and of course,

326
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:21,740
could lead to loss of life and loss of millions

327
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:22,820
of dollars in machinery and so on.

328
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:23,620
So they want to be careful

329
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:25,740
about who they will consider in the training.

330
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:28,020
But in order to actually go and do this,

331
00:13:28,020 --> 00:13:31,480
you have to actually allow people with bad scores to go

332
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:34,180
and try it on a sample basis, which the military can do.

333
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:36,440
They can do all kinds of terrible things to people.

334
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:41,020
Right? So and yeah, my friend's dad, when he was in the 50s,

335
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:42,740
he was put in a room with poison gas

336
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:44,460
and they were all ordered to take off their gas masks.

337
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:45,620
They still do that.

338
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:46,720
They still do it.

339
00:13:46,720 --> 00:13:48,000
That's part of basic training, yeah.

340
00:13:48,340 --> 00:13:48,640
All right.

341
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:54,560
Well, in that case, I had a student in 2017 who, all right,

342
00:13:54,560 --> 00:13:57,000
so they do terrible things to you, so that means

343
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
that they will actually go and put the keys to a plane

344
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,280
in the hands of someone with low IQ after giving them the training

345
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:05,360
and see, well, let's see what we can do.

346
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:06,040
All right?

347
00:14:06,040 --> 00:14:07,240
We'll see what happens.

348
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:10,260
So anyway, so first of all, of course,

349
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:12,660
there is this general line and that's very well accepted.

350
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:14,400
But now, here's the interesting question.

351
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:16,500
So this is the usual line.

352
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:20,220
Now, suppose you belong to a group against whom the test is

353
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:20,800
culturally biased.

354
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:26,000
Or suppose that the IQ test is culturally biased against you.

355
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,120
Well, where will your performance line be?

356
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:32,200
Yeah, so, so here we are.

357
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:33,740
Yes, that is the correct answer.

358
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:34,660
So rational explanations.

359
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:36,800
Almost everyone thinks, no, no, no, no.

360
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:38,860
If the test is biased against you, your performance will be low.

361
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,660
Wrong. Because, yeah, so this is biased against.

362
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:51,020
This means that if a test is unfairly hard for you,

363
00:14:51,280 --> 00:14:53,940
you will be better at doing stuff in the real world

364
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:55,100
than the test would suggest.

365
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:58,160
Just like imagine that the professor hates you.

366
00:14:58,880 --> 00:14:59,580
All right?

367
00:14:59,880 --> 00:15:03,220
Well, how are you going to do on the practical test

368
00:15:03,220 --> 00:15:04,800
for which you were trained if the professor hates you?

369
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:07,480
The professor is going to go and give you a bad grade

370
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:08,240
because he hates you, right?

371
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:10,220
Not all professors.

372
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:13,300
I would grade fairly even the students that I do hate,

373
00:15:13,300 --> 00:15:14,920
which is not a bad thing.

374
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:18,180
But suppose that I'm one of the bad kinds of professors.

375
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:20,100
I'm giving bad grades to the students that I hate just

376
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:20,840
because I hate them.

377
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:24,560
And then we go and someone else comes along and says, well,

378
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:27,000
how good are they at actually doing the test

379
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:27,540
that they learned?

380
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:30,400
And they would turn out to be better, they would be better

381
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:32,440
at doing the test than my grading indicated

382
00:15:32,440 --> 00:15:34,420
because I was unfairly marking them down.

383
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:36,140
Same idea.

384
00:15:36,140 --> 00:15:39,940
So if a test is biased against you, then you should do better

385
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:42,140
than your paper and pencil test indicates.

386
00:15:43,140 --> 00:15:45,840
Imagine that I gave you guys your final exam in Mandarin.

387
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:47,260
All right.

388
00:15:47,260 --> 00:15:48,700
So who here is fluent in Mandarin?

389
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:51,540
So you guys are fluent in Mandarin?

390
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:52,120
Yeah, both of you.

391
00:15:52,120 --> 00:15:57,180
So now you guys may be the best economists in the room,

392
00:15:57,540 --> 00:15:59,760
but if I gave the final exam in Mandarin,

393
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:01,720
you three would definitely be the best economists

394
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:03,020
in the room according to the test.

395
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:06,440
Everybody else would just be like, where do you start?

396
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:09,680
I don't know, I'll like try copying a few of the characters.

397
00:16:09,680 --> 00:16:11,480
Maybe that'll get me points.

398
00:16:13,060 --> 00:16:14,900
Like you wouldn't even know where to begin, right?

399
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:16,560
So that would be a good example of a test

400
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:19,440
that is culturally biased against non-Mandarin speakers.

401
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:22,460
And so that's one where if we were then going to use

402
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:25,440
that those test scores to predict your performance on,

403
00:16:25,440 --> 00:16:27,900
you know, so as an economist say, it would turn

404
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:29,740
out that greatly under-predicted performance

405
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:30,340
because most of the people

406
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:31,740
in this class would say you know nothing.

407
00:16:31,740 --> 00:16:33,500
And yet you actually know something.

408
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:34,500
All right.

409
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:37,040
So in general, the way the cultural bias works is

410
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:38,420
that the performance line of people

411
00:16:38,420 --> 00:16:40,320
against whom the test is biased should be

412
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:41,780
above the usual performance line.

413
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:44,680
Shockingly enough, almost everyone wants

414
00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:45,660
to say it's below, but no.

415
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:47,420
Below is if it's biased for you.

416
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:50,200
That would be like if your loving father is the professor

417
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:51,600
and gives you an A no matter what you do.

418
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:55,260
And then they give you the keys to the plane and then you crash

419
00:16:55,260 --> 00:16:57,400
it because your dad was the flight instructor who said,

420
00:16:57,400 --> 00:16:59,980
he's the best pilot ever, my son, that's my boy.

421
00:16:59,980 --> 00:17:03,180
All right, but that's how you get your kid killed, right?

422
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:06,520
Is you go, so you shouldn't do that.

423
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:07,220
So for his own sake.

424
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:10,720
But in any case, so this is what we should be theoretically

425
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:13,920
looking for if IQ tests are culturally biased

426
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:15,420
against groups that perform poorly on them.

427
00:17:15,420 --> 00:17:20,460
And the actual result is not that.

428
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:23,380
So the general result is

429
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:25,400
that actually there's just a single regression line.

430
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:27,860
And people who belong to groups who do poorly

431
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:31,520
on IQ tests do no better than you expect on practical tests.

432
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:34,360
In fact, they may do a little bit worse.

433
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:37,480
In other words, it may be the tests are actually biased

434
00:17:37,480 --> 00:17:38,840
against the groups that do well on them.

435
00:17:40,660 --> 00:17:44,520
But in any case, there's really no sign of cultural bias

436
00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:45,380
in these tests.

437
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:48,520
Instead, everybody seems to be on this line as long

438
00:17:48,520 --> 00:17:50,520
as they are native speakers of the language

439
00:17:50,860 --> 00:17:51,560
that the test is in.

440
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:55,520
So, you know, so which means, you know,

441
00:17:55,520 --> 00:17:56,900
like so cultural bias doesn't exist.

442
00:17:56,900 --> 00:17:58,200
It totally does exist.

443
00:17:58,200 --> 00:17:59,880
It just doesn't explain what people want to,

444
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:00,800
what people think it does.

445
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:04,760
It would explain, say, why if we went and gave people an IQ test

446
00:18:04,760 --> 00:18:07,160
in a foreign language, then it wouldn't predict the performance.

447
00:18:07,460 --> 00:18:08,480
So that's cultural bias.

448
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:09,320
And it's totally real.

449
00:18:09,800 --> 00:18:11,440
But it's also not what people are complaining about.

450
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:12,720
I've never heard anyone say, you know what's wrong

451
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:13,480
with these IQ tests?

452
00:18:13,480 --> 00:18:15,560
If you give them to someone who doesn't speak a language,

453
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:16,780
it doesn't predict very well.

454
00:18:18,060 --> 00:18:20,040
Well, who on earth would think that it would?

455
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:24,380
Really dumb to test people in a language they don't speak.

456
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:27,640
Okay. All right.

457
00:18:27,860 --> 00:18:30,280
So that is the big test of cultural bias.

458
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:32,900
And again, this is something where as far as I can tell

459
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:35,200
from my review of the literature,

460
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:37,680
everyone who knows the data says that's basically right.

461
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:41,480
Okay. All right.

462
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:47,360
Now, another big complaint about intelligence can be called the

463
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:50,080
multiple intelligences complaint,

464
00:18:50,620 --> 00:18:53,440
most associated with the Harvard psychologist Howard Gardner.

465
00:18:54,760 --> 00:18:54,960
All right.

466
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:58,480
And also associated with the X for dummies book series.

467
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:03,080
Right? You ever seen like golf for dummies, pool for dummies,

468
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:04,740
politics for dummies?

469
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:06,520
So the idea is we're all dummies.

470
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:09,580
We sat down and read this book for dummies

471
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:10,820
and then we're no longer dummies.

472
00:19:10,820 --> 00:19:13,160
Right? Or anyway, this is going to be something

473
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:15,060
that says something like, look, there's no one thing

474
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:15,960
that constitutes intelligence.

475
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:18,220
Everyone's good at some things, bad at other things.

476
00:19:18,220 --> 00:19:19,540
It's the idea.

477
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:20,520
All right.

478
00:19:20,920 --> 00:19:22,580
Now, trivially this is probably true.

479
00:19:22,580 --> 00:19:24,560
So you can probably find one area

480
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:27,660
where Albert Einstein was cognitively uninformed.

481
00:19:27,660 --> 00:19:29,480
Like maybe he didn't know much about cooking or something

482
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:29,840
like that.

483
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:31,040
So he gave him a test of cooking.

484
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:32,400
He'd give you a bunch of bad answers.

485
00:19:33,120 --> 00:19:37,660
But still, the fact that we see is that for a very wide range

486
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:39,840
of problems that we think of as mental problems,

487
00:19:39,840 --> 00:19:43,580
people who do good at some of them usually do good

488
00:19:43,580 --> 00:19:44,040
at all of them.

489
00:19:45,460 --> 00:19:49,760
So there is a clear positive correlation between performance

490
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:52,620
on almost every cognitive test that anyone's ever come up with.

491
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:55,940
In fact, let's see, when I was having lunch

492
00:19:55,980 --> 00:19:58,460
with famed intelligence researcher Steve Ceci,

493
00:19:58,460 --> 00:20:12,440
I said is there any exception?


